B4. Eisenstein factorisation of 2^9 - 7^3:
      2^9 - 7^3 = 169 = 13^2; (2^3 - 7) Phi_3(8,7) = 169; 8 - 7 zeta == (3 - zeta)^2 mod Phi_3: True; 8 - 7 zeta^2 == (3 - zeta^2)^2: True; N(3 - zeta) = 9 + 3 + 1 = 13
      13 = 1 mod 3 splits in Z[zeta_3]; (3 - zeta)(3 - zeta^2) = 13, so the twisted clocks are squares of the two primes above 13: HOLDS.

B5. Beal shadow. THM-4516(5)/note 2.5 conclude: 'Beal implies no map py +- m^r (r >= 3) has a free mixed shape (K,X)
    with K >= 3, sX >= 3, X >= 2'. Freeness needs only (2^K - p^X) | m^r (THM-4484), NOT 2^K - p^X = +-m^r. Counterexamples:
      3y+125 = 3y+5^3: clock 2^5 - 3^3 = 5 divides d, so shape (5,3) is free (K=5, sX=3, r=3); necklaces -> integer cycles [('00111', 475, 5, True), ('01011', 575, 5, True)]; the clock 5 is not +-m^r, no Beal solution involved
      3y+103823 = 3y+47^3: clock 2^7 - 3^4 = 47 divides d, so shape (7,4) is free (K=7, sX=4, r=3); necklaces -> integer cycles [('0001111', 143585, 7, True), ('0010111', 196601, 7, True), ('0011011', 187765, 7, True), ('0011101', 161257, 7, True), ('0101011', 223109, 7, True)]; the clock 47 is not +-m^r, no Beal solution involved
    -> the sentence is FALSE as stated; the true consequence of Beal is only: no perfect-power CLOCK 2^K - p^{sX} = +-m^r with K, sX, r >= 3.

B6. The (4,3,17) 'clock shadow is empty' sentence. A clock with exponent multiset {4,3,17} means 2^K = q^X +- m^r
    with {K, sX, r} = {4, 3, 17}. The note's reason ('no term can be a pure power of two while the other two are powers
    of one odd base') is not an argument: a coprime solution of x^4 + y^3 = z^17 has exactly one even variable, and
    the clock shadow is the sub-case where that variable is a power of 2 (e.g. x = 2: 16 = z^17 - y^3). Finite checks:
      2^K = u^a + v^b with u, v odd, {K,a,b} = {4,3,17}: solutions [] (finite search complete)
      2^K = u^a - v^b or v^b - u^a with u odd < 3000: solutions [] (bounded search; NOT a proof of emptiness)
    -> 'Its clock shadow is empty in any case' is UNSUPPORTED; emptiness of the shadow is exactly as open as the (4,3,17)
       equation restricted to solutions with a power-of-two variable (plus the K = 4a readings for x = 2^a).

SECTION B VERDICT: dictionary, census (now covered for all exponents r), free cycles, Eisenstein square: HOLD;
  the Beal-shadow consequence in THM-4516(5)/title and the 'clock shadow is empty' sentence: FAIL as stated.
