==============================================================================
SECTION D: HYP-9165 and the status-label audit
==============================================================================

D1. HYP-9165 claims (verbatim scope): the natural densities of the three 3x-1 basins and of B(5) on 3x+1 exist,
    with values 0.3269 / 0.3248 / 0.3484 and 0.938; the strict ordering dens B({5,7,10}) < dens B({1}) < dens B({17,...});
    hence a sheet-blind cycle-uniform lower bound is capped at 0.3248 rather than 1/3. Status OPEN with FINITE-EXACT support.
    session's dyadic rows [2^s,2^(s+1)) s=28..31: increments of basin {5,7,10} per doubling ['8.1e-05', '5.6e-05', '3.5e-05'],
      of basin {17,...} ['-4.2e-05', '-4.9e-05', '-4.5e-05']; basin {1} spread 4.7e-05.
    The status label OPEN is right: existence of the natural density of any Collatz basin is open on both sheets; the
    evidence supports the ORDERING to 2^32 (gaps 1.7e-3 and 2.2e-2) but the numerical VALUE 0.3248 in the title is stale:
    the {5,7,10} basin is still rising (0.32499 cumulative, 0.32505 in the top row); the hypothesis body already says
    'between 0.3250 and the limit'. A geometric extrapolation of the decaying increments (ratio ~0.7) would put the limit
    near 0.3251-0.3252; this is a heuristic, not part of the hypothesis.
    What would refute it: (a) a fourth positive 3x-1 cycle or a divergent 3x-1 orbit with positive upper density (the
    three basins then need not exhaust density 1; note the three cumulative densities sum to 1 - 2e-8 at 2^32 only because
    every n <= 2^32 was found to reach one of the three cycles); (b) a proof that some basin has different lower and
    upper densities; (c) a crossing of the {1} and {5,7,10} curves at larger N (the gap 1.7e-3 is closing at ~5e-5 per
    doubling with the increments themselves decaying, so a crossing would need the decay to stop).
    Internal inconsistency: HYP-9165's title says 'the sheet cap ... is 0.3248', its status text says 'between 0.3250 and
    the limit of the rising basin'; note section 3.1 and section 4 use 0.3248. The cap should be stated as 'the limit of
    the {5,7,10} basin density, >= 0.3250 on the evidence'.

D2. Status labels and overclaims found in the note and theorem files:
    D2.1 [THM-4516(5), note 2.5, THM-4516 title] 'Beal implies no map py +- m^r (r >= 3) has a free mixed shape (K,X) with K >= 3, sX >= 3, X >= 2'
        -> FALSE: freeness needs only (2^K - p^X) | m^r; 3y+125 has the free shape (5,3) with clock 5 | 125 (section B5). Correct statement: Beal implies no perfect-power CLOCK with K, sX, r >= 3.
    D2.2 [note 2.5, THM-4516(5)] 'Its clock shadow is empty in any case: no term of x^4 + y^3 = z^17 can be a pure power of two while the other two are powers of one odd base'
        -> UNSUPPORTED: no argument is given and none is elementary; the shadow is the set of solutions with a power-of-two variable (section B6). Should be marked OPEN/UNVERIFIED like the statement itself.
    D2.3 [note 3.2(iv), THM-4517] 'e_i ... sum to 1 if and only if almost every orbit reaches 1'
        -> Only 'if' is proved; 'only if' needs dens B(2^(2M+1)) -> 0 (tightness), not proved. Replace 'if and only if' by 'if' (converse open).
    D2.4 [note 3.2(iv)] 'B(4^i) = union_{i' >= i} B(a_{i'})'
        -> index slip: a_i maps to 2^(2i-1) < 4^i, so B(4^i) = trunk-above u union_{i' >= i+1} B(a_{i'}) (section C1).
    D2.5 [note 3.2(iv)] 'B(5) and B(32) partition the positive integers minus the powers of two'
        -> equivalent to the Collatz conjecture as stated (B(5) u B(32) u {1,2,4,8,16} is the set of n reaching 1, and 32, 64, ... lie in B(32)); replace by 'partition B(1) minus {1,2,4,8,16}'.
    D2.6 [note 3.3, THM-4517(3)] 'e_3 = e_6 = e_9 = e_12 = 0 exactly'
        -> the counts are the doubling-ray lengths (26, 20, 14, 8 up to 2^30), i.e. densities ~1e-8 that round to 0.0000000; 'exactly' should be 'to seven decimals (ray lengths 26, 20, 14, 8)'. THM-4517's 'exact check of (1)' is a check that these basins are rays.
    D2.7 [note 3.3, THM-4517(3)] 'the third [basin] is flat to four decimals from 2^20 on' / 'flat at 0.3483 to four decimals'
        -> the dyadic rows range 0.34804..0.34854: flat to THREE decimals (0.348).
    D2.8 [note 1.8, THM-4515(5)] 'PROVED lower bound of order 1/log m'
        -> the second-moment bound is exact for each m computed (<= 10^4; I extend to 3e4); the growth E[N_3^2] = O(log m) for all m is not proved in the note (local-CLT heuristic), so 'of order 1/log m' is PROVED only for the computed m. The slope is ~0.81 per unit log m, not 0.93.
    D2.9 [note 1.8] 'E[N_3] -> sqrt(3)/(2 pi rho(1-rho)) (... 1.1847 at rho = log_3 2)'
        -> 1.1847 is the value at rho = 12/19 (the shape used); at rho = log_3 2 exactly it is 1.1839.
    D2.10 [note 1.2] 'The j twisted clocks 2^m - zeta^{-k} q^x are the eigenvalues'
        -> the eigenvalues of 2^m P - q^x I are 2^m zeta^k - q^x = zeta^k (2^m - zeta^{-k} q^x); equal up to a unit (harmless).
    D2.11 [note 1.5] '|log(n'/n)| <= X|d|/(2(q n_min - |d|))'
        -> needs the implicit hypothesis q n_min > |d| (fails for 3x+11 through 1 and 3x+25 through 7, where the right side is negative); add 'for q n_min > |d|'.
    D2.12 [note 1.9] 'minimum -14.81', 'minima range over [-27.10, -14.81]', 'minimum element exactly -17'
        -> these are the elements of least absolute value (-c_min/139, the maximum of a negative cycle); the true minima range over [-237.0, -72.9]. Say 'least-absolute-value element'.
    D2.13 [note 2.2 table] '21 = 7 x (3x+49 ...) scaled'
        -> should read '21 = 7 x 3, the 13x+49 cycle through 3 scaled by 7'.
    D2.14 [THM-4516 census method (power_clocks.py)] gmpy2.iroot loop r <= 64; power_data returns None otherwise
        -> a perfect power whose least prime exponent exceeds 64 would have been dropped from the q <= 201 / q = 3 censuses; my sieve covers every prime r <= log_3 N and finds nothing new, so the census statement HOLDS (method gap closed here).
    D2.15 [note 2.5 / THM-4516(5)] 'smallest open Beal signature (3,5,7)' alongside 'among (3,4,n) only n = 4, 5 are solved'
        -> internally consistent only if 'smallest' is by largest exponent (or if (3,4,6), (3,4,7), ... count as solved by reduction); under lexicographic or reciprocal-sum order (3,4,6) or (3,4,17) would precede (3,5,7). CITED content not checked (no browsing); the ordering sense should be stated.

D3. Labels that are correct as used: PROVED for THM-4515(1)-(4) and THM-4517(1)-(2)(iii); FINITE-EXACT for the censuses and
    tables (all reproduced); CITED for Darmon-Granville, the ten Fermat-Catalan solutions, Krasikov-Lagarias, Barina; EMPIRICAL
    for the ~2.2/log m law; UNVERIFIED for the (4,3,17) statement; OPEN for HYP-9165. 'Collatz OPEN' is stated throughout.
